  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes how a surface-bound molecular modifier can tune copper for electrochemical CO2 reduction toward C2 products. A schematic copper surface is shown with a gradient in oxidation state from Cu0 to Cu+, and an organic additive is anchored above the surface. CO2 is converted along a curved reaction path to C2H4, implying promotion of carbon–carbon coupling on the modified catalyst.\n\nThe central zoom-in highlights adsorbed CO on neighboring copper sites. Two kinds of CO adsorption are indicated, labeled as lower- and higher-frequency bound CO, on surface Cu atoms that include a partially positive site marked around +0.26. This suggests the modifier creates electronically distinct copper sites and alters how CO binds, bringing two CO species into an arrangement favorable for coupling.\n\nThe energy diagram on the right compares neutral and cationic copper environments. Both begin from 2*CO as the initial state, pass through a transition state, and form *OCCO as the final state. The red pathway for Cuδ+ lies below the blue-gray pathway for Cu0 at the transition state and product, indicating that positively polarized copper lowers the barrier and stabilizes the coupled intermediate. Overall, the image conveys that interfacial electronic tuning of Cu enhances CO dimerization and ethylene formation.",
